  "worked_solution": "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\frac{1}{9}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{81}+\u22ef+\\frac{1}{{9}^{n}}+\u22ef & =1+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{{9}^{2}}+\u22ef+\\frac{1}{{9}^{n}}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{9}+{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}+\u22ef+{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{n}+\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+\u22ef+{x}^{n}+\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{9}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{9})} & =1+\\frac{1}{9}+{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{3}+\u22ef+{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{n}+\u22ef \\\\ \\frac{9}{8} & =1+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{81}+\\frac{1}{729}+\u22ef+\\frac{1}{{9}^{n}}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{9}{8}.$",
